    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so with the ac part, is it because the frequency is constantly changing the direction of the electrons? and can u show me an example of how to calculate reactance if i have a frequency of 60Hz and a capacitance of 10 microfarads?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, exactly! The changing frequency is the key, as it forces the electrons to constantly reverse their direction of flow relative to the capacitor's plates. If you have a frequency of $60\\text{Hz}$ and a capacitance of $10\\mu\\text{F}$ (which is $10 \\times 10^{-6}\\text{F}$), you can calculate the reactance using $X_C = \\frac{1}{2\\pi f C}$. Plugging in the values, you get $X_C = \\frac{1}{2\\pi (60\\text{Hz}) (10 \\times 10^{-6}\\text{F})}$, which results in approximately $26.53\\ \\Omega$. This calculation tells you how much the capacitor is resisting the $60\\text{Hz}$ AC signal.",
